<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indo-Scythians</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE–4th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Indo-Scythian, Indo-Scythian kingdoms</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> were Saka groups who moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> and Gandhara into north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from the late second century BCE. Kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maues">Maues</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azes-i">Azes I</a> ruled Gandhara and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in the first century BCE, and Saka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> dynasties, notably the Western Kshatrapas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, lasted until about 400 CE. Their coins and inscriptions mixed Greek, Indian and Iranian traditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indo-scythians.jpg" alt="Indo-Scythians" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan.Contouring and city names: DLommes · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Indo-Scythians.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
